1
00:00:08,923 --> 00:00:09,544
Very good.

2
00:00:10,285 --> 00:00:12,247
I trust you've had a fruitful day.

3
00:00:12,268 --> 00:00:15,571
I was going to say happy, but I hope it has been happy.

4
00:00:15,612 --> 00:00:23,721
Namo tassa bhagavato arahato samma sambodasa.

5
00:00:24,602 --> 00:00:32,771
Namo tassa bhagavato arahato samma sambodasa.

6
00:00:33,545 --> 00:00:50,801
I just want to knock something off here.

7
00:00:51,280 --> 00:00:51,741
That's it.

8
00:00:53,003 --> 00:00:53,743
That's better.

9
00:00:53,762 --> 00:00:58,747
Good.

10
00:00:58,768 --> 00:00:59,107
Right.

11
00:01:00,149 --> 00:01:03,332
So we had a break yesterday.

12
00:01:03,768 --> 00:01:04,790
for the moon day.

13
00:01:04,829 --> 00:01:11,862
The next discourse I want to look at is a discourse that's written about this man called Upali.

14
00:01:12,543 --> 00:01:15,289
And again, it touches upon karma.

15
00:01:15,689 --> 00:01:19,415
So I thought it's a big, it's a long discourse, split it into two nights.

16
00:01:19,736 --> 00:01:28,171
So I thought it might be good to just revise quickly what we've done so far on karma, then move on to this.

17
00:01:28,489 --> 00:01:37,478
So if you remember, the first thing that really connects us to what's happening now is the this, that law.

18
00:01:38,040 --> 00:01:41,772
Because that happened in the past, this happens now because that didn't happen.

19
00:01:42,040 --> 00:02:10,544
this doesn't happen now because this happens now this happens now because this didn't happen now this doesn't happen now and one was this um linear causality which gives us a feeling of fate and the other one was an immediacy a contingency which gives us a feeling when you put them both together then you get this continuous uh creativity so that was the first thing uh although that was connected to dependence origination

20
00:02:10,896 --> 00:02:16,223
It still applies, of course, to all the events that happen in our lives.

21
00:02:17,644 --> 00:02:22,629
And then we had that, oh dear me, where is it now?

22
00:02:22,710 --> 00:02:25,152
Just a minute.

23
00:02:25,173 --> 00:02:26,473
Here we are.

24
00:02:35,283 --> 00:02:36,044
Have I lost it?

25
00:02:37,445 --> 00:02:40,008
Just that general statement of

26
00:02:45,456 --> 00:02:53,343
that we are heirs to our karmas, owners of our karma, born of our karma, and so on and so forth.

27
00:02:53,364 --> 00:02:55,325
I seem to have lost the page, unfortunately.

28
00:02:55,366 --> 00:03:02,633
Trying to find it amongst 150 different discourses is going to be a bit difficult.

29
00:03:03,152 --> 00:03:14,704
But at least the Buddha's stressing there the importance of... What a shame I can't remind you of it.

30
00:03:23,509 --> 00:03:25,352
No, I can't find it.

31
00:03:26,272 --> 00:03:26,834
I'm unfortunate.

32
00:03:27,694 --> 00:03:31,179
I thought it was the next discourse along, but it's not.

33
00:03:31,740 --> 00:03:47,699
So there was this understanding that karma is something that we own, we're born of it from moment to moment, and that we have a control over it through, remember, this process of will.

34
00:03:48,159 --> 00:03:49,602
So if we don't will

35
00:03:50,070 --> 00:04:11,437
something an idea or something to something that we say or something that we do then of course that habit which is the karmic response to karmic consequence of past of the past actions becomes it comes it dies out and if we reinforce those

36
00:04:11,653 --> 00:04:37,982
um actions or habits that we feel are wholesome and we do it we reinforce it through the way we think the way we speak and of course our actions so that's the karmic thing and then there was this problem of what's your personal karma so that's really almost impossible to tell because there's so many factors that come into any present moment remember there was the all the business of um

37
00:04:39,007 --> 00:04:49,564
the earth, physics and chemistry, whatever happens, tsunamis, earthquakes and all that, and the good weather we've had and now it's horrible.

38
00:04:51,067 --> 00:04:54,031
All that is not our personal karma.

39
00:04:54,673 --> 00:05:02,485
And it's the same with the seed, with the body we've received from our parents.

40
00:05:02,564 --> 00:05:04,408
That's not personal karma.

41
00:05:04,648 --> 00:05:07,091
What we do with it is, but the actual

42
00:05:07,324 --> 00:05:12,716
physicality of our nature is something that's been given to us through past generations.

43
00:05:13,358 --> 00:05:14,821
And then we had the mind.

44
00:05:15,242 --> 00:05:17,807
So the mind also has a way of working.

45
00:05:18,709 --> 00:05:22,137
Certain programs are set, like, for instance, the ability to learn language.

46
00:05:22,879 --> 00:05:26,666
We also mentioned this whole business of social conditioning.

47
00:05:26,916 --> 00:05:49,649
so that is uh we we are as children conditioned to think and feel like our society like our nation whatever it is so we carry within us already all those vices and virtues that belong to our our ethnic group to our uh nation and so on and so forth

48
00:05:49,968 --> 00:06:18,404
and the problem there is that when we see something is unwholesome to be quick not to indulge it as a thought pattern definitely not to speak it and of course not to act upon it so this was that whole business of social karma it's still very it's our what's individual is how it manifests through us and we're responsible for that uh and then there was karma of course which is this

49
00:06:18,654 --> 00:06:28,608
ethical part of our nature, how we relate to the world, to other beings, to nature, to selves, and so on.

50
00:06:28,629 --> 00:06:37,221
So that relationship there, the Buddha says, is absolutely key to how we will enjoy life or not.

51
00:06:38,021 --> 00:06:47,295
And, you know, if you give credence, some credence to future lives, it has an effect on

52
00:06:47,478 --> 00:06:52,629
how we will be reborn, definitely throughout our lives.

53
00:06:53,250 --> 00:07:06,380
And not to confuse happiness, emotional happiness, which comes and goes with some deep meaningfulness in our lives, which gives us a true inner joy.

54
00:07:06,935 --> 00:07:18,471
So that's why, you know, people will sacrifice their lives for their religion, for their political, lots of political prisoners, because that is what gives their life meaning.

55
00:07:19,112 --> 00:07:20,072
And that's what's crucial.

56
00:07:21,334 --> 00:07:31,288
Franco Logotherapy, I can't remember his first name, but he was the one who survived the concentration camps.

57
00:07:31,622 --> 00:07:56,432
and through it developed a therapy called logotherapy, which was really because he had observed in the concentration camps that those who still had meaning for their lives, they were the ones who would generally survive, whereas those who'd lost meaning, who felt their lives had become meaningless or useless, would often succumb to the conditions.

58
00:07:57,442 --> 00:07:59,223
So that's where we've got to now.

59
00:07:59,363 --> 00:08:04,968
So here we've got a slight nuance coming up on Upali.

60
00:08:06,850 --> 00:08:23,766
So on this occasion, the Naganta Nathiputa, now in the Buddhist text, he's called the Niganta, part of a group, you say, by his own people, he's called the Mahavira, the great man.

61
00:08:23,807 --> 00:08:25,108
So he's the leader of the Jains.

62
00:08:25,148 --> 00:08:26,790
He's the one who founded the Jains.

63
00:08:27,074 --> 00:08:30,336
And he's the son of Nataputta.

64
00:08:30,357 --> 00:08:36,202
And he's staying in Nalanda, which became a huge university, with an assembly there.

65
00:08:36,903 --> 00:08:44,029
And there was a particular Nigantha, one of the order members, called Diga Tapasi.

66
00:08:44,370 --> 00:08:47,393
Diga means tall, so he was known as Tall Tapasi.

67
00:08:48,474 --> 00:08:54,820
And he was wandering for alms around there when he came across the Blessed One who was sitting in Pavarika's mango grove.

68
00:08:55,828 --> 00:09:03,018
And so after they exchanged greetings, etc., he took a low seat and sat to the side.

69
00:09:03,077 --> 00:09:05,520
So in other words, he's honoring the Blessed One.

70
00:09:05,941 --> 00:09:19,136
And the Blessed One asks him, Tapasi, how many kinds of action does the Nātaputta describe for the performance of evil action, for the perpetration of an evil action?

71
00:09:19,419 --> 00:09:24,167
So in other words, what sort of action, right?

72
00:09:24,307 --> 00:09:29,553
So he's thinking about mind, speech, or body would create an evil action.

73
00:09:30,294 --> 00:09:39,467
So Nigantha, so Tipasi replies, the Nigantha is not accustomed to use the word action, action.

74
00:09:39,989 --> 00:09:44,634
So this is the Buddha's word, kamma, an action, a doing.

75
00:09:45,442 --> 00:09:50,168
The Nigantha and Atapu is accustomed to the description of rod, rod.

76
00:09:50,610 --> 00:09:54,797
Now, the word for rod is dangda, which is a walking stick.

77
00:09:54,817 --> 00:09:56,820
But then, of course, it became a beating stick.

78
00:09:57,341 --> 00:10:01,226
So every time we do something evil, it was like we beat ourselves.

79
00:10:01,668 --> 00:10:06,033
And hence, the leader of the Jains would call them a rod.

80
00:10:07,797 --> 00:10:12,764
Then Tipasi, how many kinds of rod does the Nigantha describe?

81
00:10:13,133 --> 00:10:15,937
for the performance of evil, for the perpetration of evil.

82
00:10:17,458 --> 00:10:26,232
So, he says, the Nāganta describes three kinds of rods and they are the bodily rod, the verbal rod and the mental rod.

83
00:10:29,216 --> 00:10:42,616
So, we have to remember that Tipassi or the Nāganta taught that actions which were not intended but did harm still had karmic consequences.

84
00:10:42,884 --> 00:10:56,440
So, um, I remember for instance, uh, walking in, in the dark, fairly dark while I was at Gaia house and, uh, uh, inadvertently stood up on a snail.

85
00:10:57,301 --> 00:11:00,184
Um, and of course that crunching sound and then the squish.

86
00:11:01,485 --> 00:11:08,774
So, so of course I lifted my foot as soon as I could, but of course the, the poor snail had died or was dying.

87
00:11:08,794 --> 00:11:11,337
Uh, now.

88
00:11:11,976 --> 00:11:14,019
Of course, I didn't mean to do that.

89
00:11:14,059 --> 00:11:15,743
That was not a purposeful act.

90
00:11:15,942 --> 00:11:18,847
But that doesn't mean to say that the snail didn't suffer, you see.

91
00:11:19,248 --> 00:11:27,160
And so, in a sense, what the Niganta is arguing is that, well, you still cause suffering either by carelessness or by accident.

92
00:11:27,201 --> 00:11:27,861
It doesn't matter.

93
00:11:27,942 --> 00:11:32,489
You've caused suffering and therefore there's some karmic result, you see.

94
00:11:33,730 --> 00:11:34,572
But let's carry on.

95
00:11:34,591 --> 00:11:35,232
He says, well,

96
00:11:35,618 --> 00:11:45,591
How then, Tipassi, the Buddha asks, is the bodily rod, the verbal rod, another, and the mental rod still another?

97
00:11:45,731 --> 00:11:51,698
So this is the meaning that whether you mean it or not, you still have the consequence.

98
00:11:52,640 --> 00:11:58,128
The bodily rod is one, friend Gautama, the verbal rod is another, and the mental rod is another.

99
00:11:59,168 --> 00:12:05,557
Of these three kinds of rod, Tipassi, thus analyzed and distinguished which kind of rod

100
00:12:05,807 --> 00:12:16,879
does the Nigamtha describe as the most reprehensible for the performance of evil action, for the perpetration of evil action, the bodily rod, the verbal rod, or the mental rod?

101
00:12:17,659 --> 00:12:35,359
Of these three kinds of actions, friend Gautama, he describes the bodily rod, the actions by the body, as the most reprehensible for the performance of evil action, for the penetration of evil action, and not so much the verbal or the mental rod.

102
00:12:36,368 --> 00:12:42,514
So now we have this exchange, because remember, they don't have mobile phones to record anything in those days.

103
00:12:43,235 --> 00:12:45,738
Do you say bodily rod to Pasi?

104
00:12:45,758 --> 00:12:48,581
I say the bodily rod, friend Gautama.

105
00:12:49,100 --> 00:12:51,884
You said the bodily rod to Pasi.

106
00:12:52,083 --> 00:12:54,567
I did say the bodily rod, friend Gautama.

107
00:12:54,927 --> 00:12:58,711
So you actually did say the bodily rod to Pasi.

108
00:12:58,730 --> 00:13:01,433
I did say the bodily rod, friend Gautama.

109
00:13:02,355 --> 00:13:05,498
Actually, I'm adding to it just to make it a bit more palatable.

110
00:13:06,254 --> 00:13:11,759
And thus the Blessed One made the Nigamtha maintain his statement up to a third time.

111
00:13:12,139 --> 00:13:15,201
So that's to be absolutely sure that this is what you mean.

112
00:13:15,241 --> 00:13:25,750
Now the Nigamtha Tapasi asked the Blessed One, and you friend Gautama, how many kinds of rod do you describe for the performance of evil action?

113
00:13:26,451 --> 00:13:34,759
Tapasi the Tathagata, remember that's how he referred to himself, the transcendent one, the one who had arrived or...

114
00:13:35,110 --> 00:13:36,673
Yes, the one who had arrived.

115
00:13:40,217 --> 00:13:43,823
The Tathagata is not accustomed to use the description rod, rod.

116
00:13:43,924 --> 00:13:47,591
The Tathagata is accustomed to use the word action, action.

117
00:13:48,030 --> 00:13:54,322
So one was danda, which is the rod for beating, beat oneself with bad actions.

118
00:13:54,562 --> 00:13:57,506
And the Buddha uses the word kamma, which means action.

119
00:13:57,908 --> 00:14:01,653
So remember, literally, kama is just the action.

120
00:14:01,993 --> 00:14:04,316
The consequence is vipaka.

121
00:14:04,836 --> 00:14:06,259
You come across those two words.

122
00:14:08,562 --> 00:14:15,630
These days, of course, we've accepted the word karma really to mean, you know, your comeuppance.

123
00:14:18,735 --> 00:14:21,097
Friend Gautama, how many kinds of action do you describe?

124
00:14:21,158 --> 00:14:25,283
There are three kinds of actions, the bodily, the verbal and the mental.

125
00:14:25,769 --> 00:14:31,859
Oh then, friend Gautama, is the bodily reaction, verbal reaction, and mental reaction still another?

126
00:14:31,918 --> 00:14:32,960
In other words, are they not?

127
00:14:33,360 --> 00:14:39,009
Are they separate actions like in the Jain system?

128
00:14:39,029 --> 00:14:43,116
And the bodily action is one, the verbal action is another, and the mental action is still another.

129
00:14:43,717 --> 00:14:52,772
But of these three kinds of actions, thus analyzed and distinguished, which kind of action do you describe as the most reprehensible to the performance of evil action?

130
00:14:53,258 --> 00:14:54,760
penetration of evil action.

131
00:14:54,881 --> 00:14:57,246
Is it the bodily, the verbal, or the mental?

132
00:14:57,969 --> 00:15:06,828
Of these three kinds of actions Tifasi thus analyzed and distinguished, I describe the mental action as the most reprehensible for the performance of evil.

133
00:15:07,855 --> 00:15:10,638
Did you say mental action, friend Gautama?

134
00:15:10,798 --> 00:15:12,359
I did say mental action.

135
00:15:12,659 --> 00:15:16,023
Do you say the mental action, friend Gautama?

136
00:15:16,302 --> 00:15:17,924
I say the mental action.

137
00:15:17,945 --> 00:15:24,511
So you say that the mental action is the cause of the performance of evil.

138
00:15:25,091 --> 00:15:28,816
Friend Gautama, I did say it three times, you see.

139
00:15:28,895 --> 00:15:36,023
Thus did he make the blessed one maintain his statement up to the third time, after which he rose from his seat

140
00:15:36,356 --> 00:15:39,539
and went to the Ngantha Nathaputta.

141
00:15:39,559 --> 00:15:41,642
So he left after that.

142
00:15:41,662 --> 00:15:44,183
He didn't get into conversation any further.

143
00:15:45,625 --> 00:15:51,309
Now, on that occasion, the Ngantha was seated together with a large assembly of lay people from Balaka.

144
00:15:51,750 --> 00:15:54,113
And the prominent among them was Upali.

145
00:15:54,952 --> 00:16:01,980
And the Ngantha Nathaputta saw the Ngantha Digha Tapasi coming and said, now, where have you come from in the middle of the day?

146
00:16:03,400 --> 00:16:05,722
I'm coming from the presence of the recluse Gautama.

147
00:16:06,208 --> 00:16:08,591
Did you have conversation with the recluse Gautama?

148
00:16:08,912 --> 00:16:10,653
I did have some conversation.

149
00:16:10,994 --> 00:16:13,116
What was your conversation with him, Tapasi?

150
00:16:13,716 --> 00:16:18,383
Then Tapasi relates to Nigantha the entire conversation.

151
00:16:18,962 --> 00:16:30,716
And then Nigantha says, Tapasi has answered the recluse Gautama like a well-taught disciple who understands his teacher's dispensation rightly.

152
00:16:31,456 --> 00:16:35,000
What does the trivial mental rod count for

153
00:16:35,369 --> 00:16:39,474
in comparison with the gross bodily rod.

154
00:16:39,494 --> 00:16:48,783
In other words, from Ngantha's point of view, a mental thought doesn't have any big effect outside the mind.

155
00:16:49,164 --> 00:16:55,030
So how can you compare it to a physical action, which obviously has a greater effect?

156
00:16:57,234 --> 00:17:04,261
On the contrary, the bodily rod is the most reprehensible for the performance of evil, for the perpetration of evil action.

157
00:17:04,816 --> 00:17:23,673
Now, when this was said, the householder Hupali said to the Ngantha, good Venerable Sir, the Venerable Tapasi has answered the recluse Gautama like a well-taught disciple who understands the teaching of the Buddha, who understands the teaching of your dispensation.

158
00:17:24,653 --> 00:17:30,118
What does the trivial rod count for in comparison to the gross bodily rod?

159
00:17:30,138 --> 00:17:34,282
Now, Venerable Sir, I shall go and refute the

160
00:17:34,734 --> 00:17:39,000
recluse Gautama, Gautama's doctrine on the basis of this statement.

161
00:17:39,922 --> 00:18:03,914
If the recluse Gautama maintains before me what the venerable Tapasi made him maintain, then just as a strong man, now you've heard this before, might seize a long head rammed by the hair and drag him to and drag him fro and drag him around about, so in debate I will drag the recluse Gautama to and drag him fro and drag him all about.

162
00:18:04,434 --> 00:18:18,952
So that's where I'm going to leave it for tonight because tomorrow night we have this wonderful conversation with Impali and I shall leave you in suspense as to what happens by the end of it.

163
00:18:18,972 --> 00:18:23,878
So I can only hope these little readings are of some assistance.

164
00:18:23,898 --> 00:18:26,342
Do not cause even greater confusion.

165
00:18:26,727 --> 00:18:35,630
and that they inspire you to continue your practice so you are fully liberated sooner rather than later.

166
00:18:36,913 --> 00:18:38,356
So we can do a little bit of practice.

